INSERT INTO public.tac_academy_courses (slug,title,lessons,questions,answer_key) VALUES
('ricp-prep','Retirement Income Planning · RICP® Prep','[{"id":"start","title":"Start here and see the learning path","html":"<h3>Start here and see the learning path</h3><p>The American College identifies RICP 353 and 354 as required, then one elective: RICP 355 or HS 326. Study both electives here or take the route that best fits your goals.</p><p>Begin with the first module. Read the instruction and work the example before opening its solution. Apply the same steps to the new question, then complete the case and submit your graded review. Academy study is independent. Official coursework, exams, experience and designation decisions belong to the issuing organization.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#module-1\">353 · Income planning and risk</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-2\">354 · Sources of retirement income</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-3\">355 · Managing retirement income</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-4\">326 · Alternative elective: retirement needs</a></li></ol><p><a href=\"#videos\">Watch and discuss</a> · <a href=\"#capstone\">Applied case</a> · <a href=\"#workbook\">Advisor worksheet</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.theamericancollege.edu/learn/professional-designations-certifications/ricp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official credential curriculum</a> · <a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/retirement-plans/retirement-topics-required-minimum-distributions-rmds\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Practice reference</a></p>"},{"id":"videos","title":"Watch and discuss","html":"<h3>Watch and discuss</h3><p><a href=\"https://www.cfp.net/ethics/compliance-resources/2019/10/applying-the-code-and-standards-to-a-rollover-recommendation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CFP Board · Rollover recommendation case</a></p><p>Identify the plan terms and conflicts you would verify.</p>"},{"id":"module-1","title":"353 · Income planning and risk","html":"<h3>353 · Income planning and risk</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-1-1\">Build the retirement income baseline</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-1-2\">Sequence risk and spending guardrails</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-1-3\">Use real and nominal dollars consistently</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-1-1","title":"Build the retirement income baseline","html":"<h3>Build the retirement income baseline</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Start with recurring spending, irregular expenses and taxes. Split essential and discretionary needs. Subtract reliable income after expressing all figures in today’s dollars or all in future dollars. The gap is what investments or another income source must cover. A starting withdrawal percentage measures the first year only, and a plan must model longevity and changing spending.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A household needs $96,000 and has $57,000 of dependable income. Its $39,000 gap against $975,000 of investable assets implies a 4% starting draw. At 3.5%, the initial asset estimate is about $1.114 million. Test a surviving spouse’s budget independently.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>If dependable income falls by $10,000 and spending stays constant, what is the initial draw rate on $975,000?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>About 5.03%. The $49,000 gap divided by $975,000 is about 0.0503.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-1-2","title":"Sequence risk and spending guardrails","html":"<h3>Sequence risk and spending guardrails</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>When losses occur early and withdrawals continue, fewer assets remain to recover. Compare fixed real spending with guardrails that define when to cut discretionary withdrawals or permit increases. Model baseline, early loss and high inflation. Discuss whether the client can actually implement the proposed cuts.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A $1 million portfolio with $40,000 of annual withdrawals falls 20% before the first withdrawal. The requested draw is now 5%. Cutting $8,000 restores a 4% draw on $800,000, assuming dependable income stays constant.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>At $800,000, what draw rate results if withdrawals are reduced to $36,000?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>4.5%. Divide $36,000 by $800,000.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-1-3","title":"Use real and nominal dollars consistently","html":"<h3>Use real and nominal dollars consistently</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Translate a nominal return into purchasing-power terms by dividing its growth factor by inflation’s growth factor. Apply a real return to a today-dollar spending plan. If you inflate future expenses instead, discount with a nominal return. Check whether specific expenses such as care might rise faster than the household average.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>With 6% nominal growth and 3% inflation, real growth is 1.06 ÷ 1.03 − 1, or 2.91%. A $60,000 expense that inflates 3% costs $61,800 next year before changes in consumption.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>At 5% nominal growth and 4% inflation, what is the approximate real return?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>0.96%. Use 1.05 ÷ 1.04 − 1.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-2","title":"354 · Sources of retirement income","html":"<h3>354 · Sources of retirement income</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-1\">Coordinate earnings, Social Security and pensions</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-2\">Annuities, portfolio distributions and tax buckets</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-2-1","title":"Coordinate earnings, Social Security and pensions","html":"<h3>Coordinate earnings, Social Security and pensions</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Build a month-by-month bridge from last paycheck to pension and benefit start dates. Compare each claiming option with its own income, survivor outcome and required portfolio withdrawals. A pension election trades benefit size and survivor protection under the actual plan. Review healthcare enrollment separately from claiming income.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A couple needs $72,000 a year. Reliable income is $30,000 during a three-year bridge and $51,000 after. Portfolio draw drops from $42,000 to $21,000. Ignoring taxes and returns, reserve $126,000 for the bridge if the whole gap must come from liquid assets.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is the annual portfolio draw after reliable income rises to $51,000?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$21,000. Subtract $51,000 from $72,000.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-2-2","title":"Annuities, portfolio distributions and tax buckets","html":"<h3>Annuities, portfolio distributions and tax buckets</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Compare the income promise, survivor terms, inflation, access to principal and insurer strength. A payout rate is the annual payment divided by premium, and should not be read as investment return. For withdrawals, distinguish pretax, Roth and taxable money. Evaluate tax and liquidity effects of funding essentials with guaranteed income and protecting emergency assets.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A $200,000 premium buys an illustrative $11,000 annual payout. That is a 5.5% payout rate. On a $35,000 spending gap it leaves $24,000 for the portfolio. Before recommending it, confirm how much liquid capital remains and whether the spouse’s income changes after the first death.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>If the household annuitizes and then needs $60,000 for care, what fact should you verify?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Whether sufficient liquid assets remain and what access the contract permits.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-3","title":"355 · Managing retirement income","html":"<h3>355 · Managing retirement income</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-3-1\">Manage withdrawals, conversions and later-life risks</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-3-2\">Build the recurring retirement review</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-3-1","title":"Manage withdrawals, conversions and later-life risks","html":"<h3>Manage withdrawals, conversions and later-life risks</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Draft a withdrawal order for a baseline year, a low-income year and a shock year. Compare ordinary tax, capital gains, Medicare-related effects and cash needed to pay tax. Verify distribution eligibility and required minimum distributions with year-specific rules. Review incapacity authority, long-term care exposure and survivor expenses periodically.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An illustrative $36,000 conversion puts $20,000 into a 12% band and $16,000 into a 22% band, producing $5,920 incremental tax before other effects. If the household has only $3,000 of spare cash, compare a smaller conversion or another payment plan rather than treating tax as free.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is incremental tax if the conversion stays entirely within the $20,000 band at 12%?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$2,400 before other effects.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-3-2","title":"Build the recurring retirement review","html":"<h3>Build the recurring retirement review</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Assign a documented review date and triggers for income changes, loss, health events and changes to beneficiaries. Recalculate cash reserves and draw rates before making a trade. Compare baseline and stress case after any material change. Update implementation steps and follow-up responsibilities.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A couple’s baseline is $42,000 from the portfolio. After a spouse dies, expenses drop by $12,000 but reliable income drops by $19,000. The portfolio draw rises to $49,000 under the simplified model. Revisit asset allocation, taxes, liquidity and care independently.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is the net change in portfolio need?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>An increase of $7,000, since income fell more than expenses.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-4","title":"326 · Alternative elective: retirement needs","html":"<h3>326 · Alternative elective: retirement needs</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-4-1\">Elective alternative: plan for retirement needs</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-4-1","title":"Elective alternative: plan for retirement needs","html":"<h3>Elective alternative: plan for retirement needs</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>This elective pathway emphasizes accumulation and employer plans. Compare contributions, matching, vesting, fees, eligibility and withdrawal access. Keep inflation assumptions consistent and stress a savings shortfall. Review plan documents rather than applying one employer’s rules to another.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A worker earns $80,000 and the plan matches 50% of employee contributions up to 6% of pay. Contributing $4,800 earns a modeled $2,400 employer match before plan-specific constraints. If the worker contributes only $3,200, the match is $1,600.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What extra match does the worker obtain by raising contributions from $3,200 to $4,800?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$800, assuming the stated match is earned.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"capstone","title":"The Marquez retirement transition","html":"<h3>The Marquez retirement transition</h3><p>Annual spending is $100,000 including taxes. For the first two years, reliable income is $42,000; afterward it is $64,000. Investable assets start at $1.05 million. Assume a 20% decline before the first withdrawal in the shock scenario.</p><h4>Your assignment</h4><p>Build a two-year bridge and steady-state plan. Calculate both draw rates before and after the shock, then propose a spending response. Identify two missing facts about survivor income and liquidity.</p><details><summary>Reveal the worked solution</summary><p>The bridge draw is $58,000 per year, or $116,000 across two years before returns. The steady gap is $36,000. Starting rates are 5.52% and 3.43%. After a decline to $840,000 they are 6.90% and 4.29%. Compare reserve funding and flexible expenses. Verify survivor benefit amounts and access to cash before proposing a contract or transfer.</p></details><h4>Self-assessment</h4><ol><li>Check that the facts and assumptions are stated.</li><li>Show every calculation and verify the units.</li><li>Compare an alternative and explain what would change your choice.</li><li>Assign the next action and document its owner.</li></ol>"},{"id":"workbook","title":"Advisor worksheet and study plan","html":"<h3>Advisor worksheet and study plan</h3><p>Copy these fields into the fictional case file. Record source, date, responsible person and unresolved items for every entry.</p><p>Spending floor, bridge years, income sources, liquid reserve, portfolio gap, taxes and review triggers</p><p>Use one pass to study each module and do its checks. Use a second pass to solve the capstone without notes and explain every missed graded question. Use the issuer’s current course and exam details when planning formal enrollment.</p>"}]','[{"prompt":"If dependable income falls by $10,000 and spending stays constant, what is the initial draw rate on $975,000?","choices":["About 4.10%","About 6.05%","About 5.03%","4.00%"]},{"prompt":"At $800,000, what draw rate results if withdrawals are reduced to $36,000?","choices":["3.6%","4.0%","5.0%","4.5%"]},{"prompt":"At 5% nominal growth and 4% inflation, what is the approximate real return?","choices":["0.96%","9.00%","4.00%","5.00%"]},{"prompt":"What is the annual portfolio draw after reliable income rises to $51,000?","choices":["$51,000","$21,000","$30,000","$42,000"]},{"prompt":"If the household annuitizes and then needs $60,000 for care, what fact should you verify?","choices":["Whether the annuity can always be redeemed in full","Whether the payment is always tax-free","Whether sufficient liquid assets remain and what access the contract permits","Whether payout rate equals total return"]},{"prompt":"What is incremental tax if the conversion stays entirely within the $20,000 band at 12%?","choices":["$3,600","$4,400","$5,920","$2,400 before other effects"]},{"prompt":"What is the net change in portfolio need?","choices":["An increase of $7,000, since income fell more than expenses","A decrease of $12,000","A decrease of $7,000","No change"]},{"prompt":"What extra match does the worker obtain by raising contributions from $3,200 to $4,800?","choices":["$4,800","$800, assuming the stated match is earned","$1,600","$2,400"]}]','CDABCDAB'),
('clu-prep','Life Insurance and Business Planning · CLU® Prep','[{"id":"start","title":"Start here and see the learning path","html":"<h3>Start here and see the learning path</h3><p>The current four-course route is HS 311, HS 323, HS 330 and HS 331. Use the legal and elective lessons as supplemental study. The issuer retired HS 324 as a standalone requirement in April 2026.</p><p>Begin with the first module. Read the instruction and work the example before opening its solution. Apply the same steps to the new question, then complete the case and submit your graded review. Academy study is independent. Official coursework, exams, experience and designation decisions belong to the issuing organization.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#module-1\">311 · Insurance foundations</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-2\">323 · Life insurance planning</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-3\">330 · Estate planning</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-4\">331 · Business owners and professionals</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-5\">324 · Supplement: legal and tax details</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-6\">Electives · Supplement: cross-domain practice</a></li></ol><p><a href=\"#videos\">Watch and discuss</a> · <a href=\"#capstone\">Applied case</a> · <a href=\"#workbook\">Advisor worksheet</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.theamericancollege.edu/learn/professional-designations-certifications/clu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official credential curriculum</a> · <a href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/life-insurance.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Practice reference</a></p>"},{"id":"videos","title":"Watch and discuss","html":"<h3>Watch and discuss</h3><p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iw1GQRSZNZw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Texas Department of Insurance · Understanding universal life</a></p><p>Identify which values in the contract are guaranteed and what can change.</p>"},{"id":"module-1","title":"311 · Insurance foundations","html":"<h3>311 · Insurance foundations</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-1-1\">Build a household risk inventory</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-1-2\">Compare health, disability and care exposures</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-1-1","title":"Build a household risk inventory","html":"<h3>Build a household risk inventory</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Estimate severity and frequency, then decide which risks to avoid, reduce, retain or transfer. Start with dependents, job income, liability and gaps in employer benefits. Compare premium, deductible, coverage conditions and capacity to absorb the retained loss. Avoid using the same reserve to cover several simultaneous losses.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A household has $12,000 available for contingencies, a $5,000 medical deductible and a $2,000 property deductible. If both covered events occur, the deductibles consume $7,000 before coinsurance, exclusions or other costs. Model income interruption separately.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>How much of the reserve remains after both stated deductibles?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$5,000 before other costs.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-1-2","title":"Compare health, disability and care exposures","html":"<h3>Compare health, disability and care exposures</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Health insurance pays eligible medical claims. Disability coverage replaces part of earnings under an occupational definition and waiting period. Long-term care benefits depend on contractual triggers and qualifying services. For a realistic protection plan, verify when payments begin, their expected amount and how long savings must bridge the gap.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A worker needs $4,000 monthly and expects $2,700 net from a group disability plan after a three-month wait. The waiting-period spending need is $12,000 without other income. Once payments begin, the ongoing gap is $1,300 monthly under the assumptions.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>How much cash does the worker need for four months covering the wait and the first month with benefits?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$13,300. Add $12,000 for the wait and $1,300 for the first benefit month.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-2","title":"323 · Life insurance planning","html":"<h3>323 · Life insurance planning</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-1\">Translate the client’s exposure into coverage</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-2\">Read illustrations, loans and replacement costs</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-3\">Compare underwriting and policy performance</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-2-1","title":"Translate the client’s exposure into coverage","html":"<h3>Translate the client’s exposure into coverage</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Identify owner, insured, beneficiary and payer. Estimate separate needs for debts, survivor income and transition costs, subtracting only assets or policies available for that goal. Match coverage duration and funding ability. Compare term, whole life, universal life and variable structures by guarantees, investment risk and lapse exposure.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A family needs $500,000 for income, $230,000 for debts and $20,000 for transition costs. It has $100,000 of usable life coverage and $50,000 of dedicated savings. Initial gap is $600,000. If the group policy ends with employment, the gap rises to $700,000.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is the modeled gap if the existing $100,000 remains but the savings are earmarked for emergencies?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$650,000. Exclude the $50,000 reserve.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-2-2","title":"Read illustrations, loans and replacement costs","html":"<h3>Read illustrations, loans and replacement costs</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Separate guaranteed and nonguaranteed columns. Review charges, surrender value, assumed rates and premium schedules. Policy loans accrue interest and can reduce net proceeds or contribute to lapse. Before replacing coverage, compare in-force terms with the issued replacement, timing, surrender costs and applicable replacement disclosures.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A policy shows $110,000 gross cash value, a $20,000 loan including accrued interest and a $6,000 surrender charge. The simplified cash available on surrender is $84,000 before any tax. Confirm the carrier’s quoted net value and basis before recommending surrender.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>If the charge disappears but the loan remains $20,000, what is simplified cash surrender value?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$90,000, before tax and other adjustments.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-2-3","title":"Compare underwriting and policy performance","html":"<h3>Compare underwriting and policy performance</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>The application and underwriting process translates risk evidence into issued coverage. Compare coverage classification, exclusions, riders and guarantees using issued policy terms. Match disability or accelerated benefits to defined events. Distinguish what the insured can qualify for today from modeled future affordability.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An illustration assumes $720 annual charges in an early policy year and $1,140 later. At $900 credited funding, the early year leaves $180 before other changes, while the later year would use $240 of other policy value, ignoring earnings. Ask the carrier for current and guaranteed projections.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What happens in the later year under the simplified assumptions?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>The $240 shortfall must come from other policy value or additional funding.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-3","title":"330 · Estate planning","html":"<h3>330 · Estate planning</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-3-1\">Ownership, transfer and estate liquidity</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-3-1","title":"Ownership, transfer and estate liquidity","html":"<h3>Ownership, transfer and estate liquidity</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>For every policy and account, record legal owner, rights, beneficiary and access timing. A death benefit payable outside probate can still raise estate inclusion questions. Evaluate funding for tax, debts and family support separately. Life trust design and transfers require coordinated legal and tax review.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An estate has $90,000 of bills and $50,000 immediately accessible. A $300,000 policy names an adult child. The estate cash gap remains $40,000 under the stated facts. Do not count policy money as estate liquidity without a valid arrangement.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What initial gap remains if another estate account supplies $25,000 before bills are due?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$15,000.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-4","title":"331 · Business owners and professionals","html":"<h3>331 · Business owners and professionals</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-4-1\">Buy-sell, key person and succession</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-4-1","title":"Buy-sell, key person and succession","html":"<h3>Buy-sell, key person and succession</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Identify the agreement’s trigger, buyer, price mechanism and funding obligation. Cross-purchase and entity purchase assign the purchase to different parties. Key person coverage protects the business from a financial loss and does not by itself transfer ownership. Compare valuation, actual policy ownership, tax and creditor questions with counsel and CPA.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>Two owners split a business worth $2.4 million under the agreement’s simplified formula. A funded buyout of one half needs $1.2 million before adjustments. If the obligated buyer owns $900,000 of available policy benefit, the modeled gap is $300,000. Confirm reviewed valuation and policy terms.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>If the company owns a policy for business interruption from the owner’s death but the other owner owes the buyout, what should be verified?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Whether the actual agreement and funding make proceeds available to the obligated buyer.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-5","title":"324 · Supplement: legal and tax details","html":"<h3>324 · Supplement: legal and tax details</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-5-1\">Contract formation, rights and claims</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-5-2\">Track premium, basis and policy changes</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-5-1","title":"Contract formation, rights and claims","html":"<h3>Contract formation, rights and claims</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Separate application, offer or counteroffer, acceptance, premium handling, receipt terms and policy delivery. A beneficiary designation directs payment, subject to the contract and law. Assignment can transfer ownership rights or secure a debt. Verify signature, capacity, ownership and rights when reviewing a change. Keep advertising, privacy and unfair-practices rules in view.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A $500,000 policy is collaterally assigned to secure a $120,000 debt. If the assignment gives the creditor priority up to that balance and there are no other adjustments, $380,000 remains for the contractual beneficiary. Review release paperwork if the debt was repaid.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What determines whether a lender receives policy proceeds after the loan has been repaid?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>The assignment, any recorded release and the governing facts and law.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-5-2","title":"Track premium, basis and policy changes","html":"<h3>Track premium, basis and policy changes</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>A policy’s basis, cash value, loans and ownership answer different questions. For surrender and policy changes, obtain an in-force ledger, carrier tax basis and loan totals. Ask whether modified endowment, transfer-for-value or exchange rules are implicated. Verify any proposed exchange’s eligibility and actual facts with tax counsel before selling a strategy as tax-free.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A policy’s carrier shows a gross cash value of $85,000 and $7,000 in surrender charges with no loans. Use $78,000 as the starting cash proceeds figure. If verified basis is $63,000 and no other factors apply, the simplified gain is $15,000.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>If charges drop to $5,000, what is the simplified gain using the same basis?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$17,000. $85,000 − $5,000 − $63,000.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-6","title":"Electives · Supplement: cross-domain practice","html":"<h3>Electives · Supplement: cross-domain practice</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-6-1\">Apply planning electives to the life insurance case</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-6-1","title":"Apply planning electives to the life insurance case","html":"<h3>Apply planning electives to the life insurance case</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>The catalog identifies electives in broad planning, health insurance, investments, retirement needs and income taxation. Use all five as lenses for comparison. Start with household goals and liquidity, examine health and disability gaps, compare insurance with an appropriate investment strategy, test retirement cash flow, and model tax effects using verified facts.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>The family can spend $1,000 monthly. A permanent proposal costs $900 and a term proposal $250 for equivalent initial coverage under their actual terms. The monthly difference is $650. Compare how the difference affects reserves and retirement, and what happens when term coverage ends. Compare guarantees, duration and future insurability.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is the annual premium difference in the example?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$7,800. Multiply $650 by 12.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"capstone","title":"The Reed ownership transition","html":"<h3>The Reed ownership transition</h3><p>Two partners own a business equally. Their agreement uses an updated $3 million equity value and requires the remaining partner to buy the deceased owner’s share. A $1 million policy owned by the company names the company. The deceased owner’s family separately needs $700,000 and has $200,000 of personal life insurance.</p><h4>Your assignment</h4><p>Compute both gaps. Trace the policy proceeds and propose what the planner, carrier, attorney and CPA should verify before restructuring.</p><details><summary>Reveal the worked solution</summary><p>The modeled buyout is $1.5 million. If the purchaser has no verified access to the company policy, do not count the $1 million as buyer funding. If an approved arrangement makes all of it available, the gap is $500,000. The personal gap is $500,000. Document ownership, policy basis, valuation, funding and agreement changes before implementing.</p></details><h4>Self-assessment</h4><ol><li>Check that the facts and assumptions are stated.</li><li>Show every calculation and verify the units.</li><li>Compare an alternative and explain what would change your choice.</li><li>Assign the next action and document its owner.</li></ol>"},{"id":"workbook","title":"Advisor worksheet and study plan","html":"<h3>Advisor worksheet and study plan</h3><p>Copy these fields into the fictional case file. Record source, date, responsible person and unresolved items for every entry.</p><p>Need, insured, owner, payer, beneficiary, current coverage, recommended structure and professional review</p><p>Use one pass to study each module and do its checks. Use a second pass to solve the capstone without notes and explain every missed graded question. Use the issuer’s current course and exam details when planning formal enrollment.</p>"}]','[{"prompt":"How much of the reserve remains after both stated deductibles?","choices":["$10,000","$12,000","$5,000 before other costs","$7,000"]},{"prompt":"How much cash does the worker need for four months covering the wait and the first month with benefits?","choices":["$12,000","$10,800","$16,000","$13,300"]},{"prompt":"What is the modeled gap if the existing $100,000 remains but the savings are earmarked for emergencies?","choices":["$650,000","$550,000","$600,000","$750,000"]},{"prompt":"If the charge disappears but the loan remains $20,000, what is simplified cash surrender value?","choices":["$110,000","$90,000, before tax and other adjustments","$84,000","$104,000"]},{"prompt":"What happens in the later year under the simplified assumptions?","choices":["Coverage is automatically renewed forever","Yearly charges fall to $900","The $240 shortfall must come from other policy value or additional funding","The policy earns $240 automatically"]},{"prompt":"What initial gap remains if another estate account supplies $25,000 before bills are due?","choices":["$25,000","$40,000","$65,000","$15,000"]},{"prompt":"If the company owns a policy for business interruption from the owner’s death but the other owner owes the buyout, what should be verified?","choices":["Whether the actual agreement and funding make proceeds available to the obligated buyer","That corporate proceeds always belong to the surviving owner","That every key-person policy is a buy-sell contract","That ownership transfers when a policy is issued"]},{"prompt":"What determines whether a lender receives policy proceeds after the loan has been repaid?","choices":["The beneficiary’s preferred order","The assignment, any recorded release and the governing facts and law","The agent’s recollection alone","The cash value projection"]},{"prompt":"If charges drop to $5,000, what is the simplified gain using the same basis?","choices":["$22,000","$80,000","$17,000","$15,000"]},{"prompt":"What is the annual premium difference in the example?","choices":["$650","$3,000","$10,800","$7,800"]}]','CDABCDABCD'),
('cic-prep','Insurance Coverage and Agency Practice · CIC Prep','[{"id":"start","title":"Start here and see the learning path","html":"<h3>Start here and see the learning path</h3><p>Explore all seven CIC subject areas. The issuer publishes its own eligible course and examination combinations and designation timelines.</p><p>Begin with the first module. Read the instruction and work the example before opening its solution. Apply the same steps to the new question, then complete the case and submit your graded review. Academy study is independent. Official coursework, exams, experience and designation decisions belong to the issuing organization.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#module-1\">Personal Lines · Personal lines</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-2\">Commercial Property · Commercial property</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-3\">Commercial Casualty · Commercial casualty</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-4\">Commercial Multiline · Commercial multiline</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-5\">Life and Health · Life and health</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-6\">Agency Management · Agency management</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-7\">Insurance Company Operations · Insurance company operations</a></li></ol><p><a href=\"#videos\">Watch and discuss</a> · <a href=\"#capstone\">Applied case</a> · <a href=\"#workbook\">Advisor worksheet</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.riskeducation.org/programs/cic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official credential curriculum</a> · <a href=\"https://tdi.texas.gov/pubs/consumer/cb021.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Practice reference</a></p>"},{"id":"videos","title":"Watch and discuss","html":"<h3>Watch and discuss</h3><p><a href=\"https://www.tdi.texas.gov/video/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Texas Department of Insurance · Auto and Home / Property videos</a></p><p>Choose a video in those sections and describe the type of loss, who can claim and where coverage would be verified.</p>"},{"id":"module-1","title":"Personal Lines · Personal lines","html":"<h3>Personal Lines · Personal lines</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-1-1\">Read household policies from the loss backward</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-1-1","title":"Read household policies from the loss backward","html":"<h3>Read household policies from the loss backward</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Identify the claimant, property, cause, timing and applicable policy section. Compare homeowners building, other structures, contents, loss of use and liability. Check limits, endorsements and flood or water exclusions. For auto, distinguish liability owed to others from first-party physical damage and medical coverage. Test umbrella attachment and excluded exposures.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An auto collision causes $6,000 of covered damage to the insured car with a $1,000 deductible. The other driver claims injury. The insured car’s first-party payment is $5,000 under the simplified terms. The injury claim requires a separate liability analysis, even though both arose from one event.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>Which coverage should be evaluated for the other driver’s injury claim?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Bodily injury liability, subject to fault and contract terms.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-2","title":"Commercial Property · Commercial property","html":"<h3>Commercial Property · Commercial property</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-1\">Value the building and model a shutdown</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-2\">Estimate restoration and continuing costs</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-2-1","title":"Value the building and model a shutdown","html":"<h3>Value the building and model a shutdown</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Identify who owns building, equipment and stock. Inspect the declarations, causes of loss, valuation, coinsurance and any waiting period. Business income models lost net income plus continuing eligible expenses during restoration. Extra expense addresses qualifying costs to keep operating.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A $1 million building has an 80% coinsurance requirement. The insured carries $600,000 against $800,000 required. On a $160,000 covered partial loss with a $5,000 deductible, the simplified estimate is 75% × $160,000 − $5,000 = $115,000.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What would the simplified settlement be if $800,000 of coverage was carried and the same deductible applied?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$155,000, assuming no policy adjustment or other limitation.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-2-2","title":"Estimate restoration and continuing costs","html":"<h3>Estimate restoration and continuing costs</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>A business-income worksheet starts from expected operations had a loss not occurred. Review income, continuing costs and how quickly operations can resume. Compare ordinary continuing payroll, restoration assumptions and temporary operating costs with policy definitions. Do not add saved expenses to lost net income.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>Over the assumed restoration window, a firm would have had $80,000 net income and $130,000 of normal expenses that continue. Assume all expenses qualify. The estimated loss is $210,000 before policy terms. A $20,000 temporary relocation expense requires a separate coverage review.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>If eligible continuing expenses are $100,000, what is the simplified business income estimate?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$180,000 before contract limits and conditions.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-3","title":"Commercial Casualty · Commercial casualty","html":"<h3>Commercial Casualty · Commercial casualty</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-3-1\">Trace liability from claim to applicable limits</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-3-2\">Analyze auto, workers and contracts separately</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-3-1","title":"Trace liability from claim to applicable limits","html":"<h3>Trace liability from claim to applicable limits</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Distinguish CGL premises, operations, products/completed operations and personal or advertising injury. Identify who is insured, exclusions, event timing, occurrence and aggregate limits. For commercial auto match each coverage to the applicable symbol. For worker injuries consider workers’ compensation and employers liability rather than assuming general liability covers the injury.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>The next covered occurrence is $600,000. Its occurrence limit is $1 million but the applicable aggregate has $350,000 left. With no other adjustment, the available stated limit is $350,000. Additional insured status and contractual requirements must be checked against the policy and endorsements.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>Which limit controls the example?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>The remaining $350,000 applicable aggregate.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-3-2","title":"Analyze auto, workers and contracts separately","html":"<h3>Analyze auto, workers and contracts separately</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Identify who owns a vehicle and why it is used. Review coverage symbols separately for liability and physical damage. Test employer exposure when workers use their cars. Consider workers’ compensation and employers liability for injury arising from work. Check the contractual risk transfer and additional insured wording rather than assuming it follows the certificate.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An employee uses a personal sedan for an employer delivery. The employee’s vehicle ownership and the employer’s liability exposure are separate. Verify the personal policy’s business-use terms and the employer’s nonowned-auto coverage, then determine the terms that apply to damage to the car.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>Which exposure usually points to the employer’s nonowned auto analysis?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Liability from the employee driving their own vehicle for company business.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-4","title":"Commercial Multiline · Commercial multiline","html":"<h3>Commercial Multiline · Commercial multiline</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-4-1\">Close gaps between policies</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-4-1","title":"Close gaps between policies","html":"<h3>Close gaps between policies</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Use an exposure matrix for equipment breakdown, inland marine, crime, cyber, excess and professional liability. Compare covered triggers and who suffers the loss. Identify where primary and excess coverage attach and verify underlying requirements. Consider specialized management and employment claims based on the actual form.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A contractor’s mobile equipment is damaged away from its main site. Separately, an employee transfers money after a fraudulent email. Review equipment floater wording for mobility and crime/cyber forms for the social-engineering event. An umbrella does not automatically fill either gap.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What should the broker check first for the fraudulent transfer?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Whether the applicable crime or cyber wording covers that specific transfer mechanism.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-5","title":"Life and Health · Life and health","html":"<h3>Life and Health · Life and health</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-5-1\">Build a protection recommendation</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-5-1","title":"Build a protection recommendation","html":"<h3>Build a protection recommendation</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Separate income replacement, medical cost, death obligations and long-term care. Quantify each gap, track ownership and beneficiaries, and compare coverage duration and exclusions. For employee benefits confirm effective dates, conversion rights and who pays premiums before modeling net payments.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A worker needs $4,800 monthly if disabled and has $2,900 of expected after-tax group benefits. The initial gap is $1,900. Add a waiting-period reserve before assuming monthly payments arrive. For life, use earmarked assets and death benefits only once.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>How much is the monthly gap if group disability is $3,500 net?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$1,300. Subtract the net benefit from stated expenses.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-6","title":"Agency Management · Agency management","html":"<h3>Agency Management · Agency management</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-6-1\">Design a workflow that produces evidence</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-6-1","title":"Design a workflow that produces evidence","html":"<h3>Design a workflow that produces evidence</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Standardize risk discovery, quote authorization, binding, evidence and renewal. Track who made each decision and when it was communicated. Record declinations and coverage alternatives. Treat the certificate as evidence and verify policy endorsements before representing rights. Build review controls for employee training, complaints and E&amp;O.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A client asks for a certificate listing a contractor as additional insured. Verify the contract requirement, check policy wording and request the applicable endorsement. Record receipt, verify effective dates and issue a certificate reflecting actual coverage. Escalate discrepancies.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>Which instrument can add contractual additional insured rights when the policy does not already grant them?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>A policy endorsement satisfying its terms.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-7","title":"Insurance Company Operations · Insurance company operations","html":"<h3>Insurance Company Operations · Insurance company operations</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-7-1\">Connect underwriting, claims and financials</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-7-1","title":"Connect underwriting, claims and financials","html":"<h3>Connect underwriting, claims and financials</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Premium pricing reflects expected loss, expenses and risk. Underwriting selects and classifies exposures. Claims investigates and pays covered losses under policy terms. Reinsurance transfers part of an insurer’s risk under a separate agreement. Compare loss ratio and expense ratio using the same stated time and accounting basis before combining them.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>If incurred losses and defined loss adjustment expenses total $6.5 million on $10 million of earned premium, the illustrative loss ratio is 65%. On a simplified comparable basis, adding 30% expenses gives a 95% combined ratio before investment income.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is the simplified combined ratio if the expense ratio rises to 37%?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>102%. Add 65% and 37%.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"capstone","title":"The Hill Country contractor renewal","html":"<h3>The Hill Country contractor renewal</h3><p>A contractor has a $1.2 million building with 80% coinsurance and a $720,000 building limit. Its equipment travels, employees use personal cars and subcontractors request certificates. A covered building loss totals $100,000 before a $5,000 deductible.</p><h4>Your assignment</h4><p>Calculate the illustrative property settlement, identify four exposures that need separate review, and write a binding and documentation checklist.</p><details><summary>Reveal the worked solution</summary><p>Required insurance is $960,000. The carried ratio is 75%, yielding $70,000 after the stated deductible. Check equipment/mobile coverage, hired and nonowned auto, liability and endorsements, and worker injuries. Capture exposure details, policy form, limits, insurer confirmations, client decisions and binding authorization.</p></details><h4>Self-assessment</h4><ol><li>Check that the facts and assumptions are stated.</li><li>Show every calculation and verify the units.</li><li>Compare an alternative and explain what would change your choice.</li><li>Assign the next action and document its owner.</li></ol>"},{"id":"workbook","title":"Advisor worksheet and study plan","html":"<h3>Advisor worksheet and study plan</h3><p>Copy these fields into the fictional case file. Record source, date, responsible person and unresolved items for every entry.</p><p>Exposure, named insured, form and edition, limit, deductible, exclusions, endorsement and binding evidence</p><p>Use one pass to study each module and do its checks. Use a second pass to solve the capstone without notes and explain every missed graded question. Use the issuer’s current course and exam details when planning formal enrollment.</p>"}]','[{"prompt":"Which coverage should be evaluated for the other driver’s injury claim?","choices":["Comprehensive","Homeowners dwelling","Bodily injury liability, subject to fault and contract terms","Collision"]},{"prompt":"What would the simplified settlement be if $800,000 of coverage was carried and the same deductible applied?","choices":["$115,000","$120,000","$160,000","$155,000, assuming no policy adjustment or other limitation"]},{"prompt":"If eligible continuing expenses are $100,000, what is the simplified business income estimate?","choices":["$180,000 before contract limits and conditions","$100,000","$210,000","$80,000"]},{"prompt":"Which limit controls the example?","choices":["The certificate of insurance limit regardless of policy","The remaining $350,000 applicable aggregate","The $1 million occurrence limit alone","The $600,000 claimed amount"]},{"prompt":"Which exposure usually points to the employer’s nonowned auto analysis?","choices":["Medical benefits for a customer alone","Cyber theft of employer funds","Liability from the employee driving their own vehicle for company business","Damage to the employer’s building"]},{"prompt":"What should the broker check first for the fraudulent transfer?","choices":["The CGL bodily injury limit","The property building limit","The inland marine transit limit","Whether the applicable crime or cyber wording covers that specific transfer mechanism"]},{"prompt":"How much is the monthly gap if group disability is $3,500 net?","choices":["$1,300","$1,900","$3,500","$8,300"]},{"prompt":"Which instrument can add contractual additional insured rights when the policy does not already grant them?","choices":["An agency email promising coverage","A policy endorsement satisfying its terms","Certificate alone","An internal checklist"]},{"prompt":"What is the simplified combined ratio if the expense ratio rises to 37%?","choices":["97%","65%","102%","95%"]}]','CDABCDABC'),
('health-underwriting-rhu-legacy','Health Underwriting and Benefits · Legacy RHU Study','[{"id":"start","title":"Start here and see the learning path","html":"<h3>Start here and see the learning path</h3><p>RHU was sunset and is closed to new students. This track teaches legacy health-underwriting and benefits topics for advisors. NABIP offers REBC as an active pathway and publishes reciprocity rules for existing holders.</p><p>Begin with the first module. Read the instruction and work the example before opening its solution. Apply the same steps to the new question, then complete the case and submit your graded review. Academy study is independent. Official coursework, exams, experience and designation decisions belong to the issuing organization.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#module-1\">Individual coverage · Individual coverage</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-2\">Group benefits · Group funding and renewals</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-3\">ACA and enrollment · ACA and enrollment</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-4\">Claims and care navigation · Claims and navigation</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-5\">Medicare and supplemental products · Medicare and supplemental coverage</a></li></ol><p><a href=\"#videos\">Watch and discuss</a> · <a href=\"#capstone\">Applied case</a> · <a href=\"#workbook\">Advisor worksheet</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.nabiptraining.org/page/rebc-reciprocity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official credential curriculum</a> · <a href=\"https://www.nabiptraining.org/nabip-training/curriculum/nabip-rebc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Practice reference</a></p>"},{"id":"videos","title":"Watch and discuss","html":"<h3>Watch and discuss</h3><p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t13f66cLHVQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Texas Department of Insurance · How to shop for health insurance</a></p><p>Explain how you would compare two networks and annual costs.</p>"},{"id":"module-1","title":"Individual coverage · Individual coverage","html":"<h3>Individual coverage · Individual coverage</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-1-1\">Compare health plans on dollars and access</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-1-1","title":"Compare health plans on dollars and access","html":"<h3>Compare health plans on dollars and access</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Inventory doctors, hospitals, prescriptions, predictable services and a high-use scenario. Premium is paid for access. Deductibles, copays and coinsurance determine eligible out-of-pocket costs. Compare total annual premiums and eligible out-of-pocket exposure, then verify network and formulary status. Short-term and fixed indemnity products use different benefits and protections from comprehensive major medical.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>Plan A costs $400 a month and has a $6,000 in-network out-of-pocket cap. Plan B costs $525 and has a $3,500 cap. In a simplified high-use scenario reaching each cap, A costs $10,800 and B $9,800. Compare both at expected use as well. Out-of-network and noncovered costs can change results.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is the high-use modeled cost difference?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Plan B is $1,000 lower under the assumptions.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-2","title":"Group benefits · Group funding and renewals","html":"<h3>Group benefits · Group funding and renewals</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-1\">Assess employer funding and renewal</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-2\">Model renewals on a comparable basis</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-2-1","title":"Assess employer funding and renewal","html":"<h3>Assess employer funding and renewal</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Collect census information securely, validate eligibility and participation, and compare fully insured, self-funded and level-funded arrangements on actual contract terms. For self-funded group plans analyze claims assumptions, reserves, stop-loss attachment and exclusions. A premium or monthly funding figure alone does not measure exposure.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An employer with 40 enrolled employees budgets $600 per employee per month. That is $288,000 annually. A stop-loss contract can reimburse specified costs above an attachment point under its terms. Review claim timing, exclusions, covered lives and renewal risk before comparing it with insured premium.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What does stop-loss typically protect under the contract?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>The employer or plan sponsor against specified claim exposure.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-2-2","title":"Model renewals on a comparable basis","html":"<h3>Model renewals on a comparable basis</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Normalize enrollment, contribution mix, claims period and benefits before comparing renewals. Per-member costs can rise even when enrollment falls. Review network changes, plan disruption and employer budget under low and high claims before presenting a recommendation.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An insured plan costs $360,000 for 50 enrolled employees. Renewal is $378,000 with 45 enrollees. Annual cost per enrolled employee changes from $7,200 to $8,400, or about 16.7%, even though total premium rises only 5%.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>Which metric isolates the pricing and mix change more effectively than total premium alone?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Cost per enrolled employee, alongside a normalized benefits comparison.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-3","title":"ACA and enrollment · ACA and enrollment","html":"<h3>ACA and enrollment · ACA and enrollment</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-3-1\">Build a coverage and compliance timeline</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-3-2\">Compare individual alternatives after a life event</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-3-1","title":"Build a coverage and compliance timeline","html":"<h3>Build a coverage and compliance timeline</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Distinguish individual enrollment from employer eligibility. For individual plans verify Marketplace income, tax household, eligibility and enrollment events against official rules. For employer plans map measurement, offer, enrollment, effective dates, notices and required reporting with a benefits specialist. Avoid assumptions based only on headcount or one payroll run.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An employee loses employer coverage at month end. Confirm actual loss date and appropriate enrollment options using current guidance. Compare COBRA, a Marketplace plan and any spouse plan for effective date, network and costs before the employee chooses.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What should be verified before telling the employee when coverage will begin?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>The qualifying event, each option’s enrollment rules and its actual effective date.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-3-2","title":"Compare individual alternatives after a life event","html":"<h3>Compare individual alternatives after a life event</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Use household facts, expected annual income, enrollment dates and coverage offers. Check Marketplace rules using HealthCare.gov and compare any employer continuation option. Explain plan-year resets and medication continuity. Treat an estimated subsidy as conditional until verified.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>Two verified options cost $440 and $580 monthly. The lower-premium option requires a new specialist and has a higher cap. The higher-premium option saves an assumed $2,000 on predictable eligible services. Its $1,680 extra annual premium is more than offset in the simplified case by $320.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What if expected covered-service savings fall to $1,000?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>The higher-premium option costs $680 more before other differences.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-4","title":"Claims and care navigation · Claims and navigation","html":"<h3>Claims and care navigation · Claims and navigation</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-4-1\">Use claim documents to resolve a billing problem</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-4-1","title":"Use claim documents to resolve a billing problem","html":"<h3>Use claim documents to resolve a billing problem</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Compare the plan’s evidence of coverage, EOB and provider bill. Identify billed charge, allowed amount, plan payment and eligible member cost. Check coding, network status and prior authorization terms. Escalate through the plan’s appeal process where warranted, with consent and limited data access.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A provider bills $1,500 but the in-network allowed amount is $900. The plan pays $720 and the EOB shows $180 member coinsurance. Compare the bill against the EOB and confirm the contract applies before treating the $600 difference as the patient’s responsibility.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is the coinsurance percent on the allowed amount?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>20%. Divide $180 by $900.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-5","title":"Medicare and supplemental products · Medicare and supplemental coverage","html":"<h3>Medicare and supplemental products · Medicare and supplemental coverage</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-5-1\">Distinguish replacement coverage from cash benefits</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-5-1","title":"Distinguish replacement coverage from cash benefits","html":"<h3>Distinguish replacement coverage from cash benefits</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Compare Original Medicare, Medicare Advantage, drug coverage and Medigap by eligibility and function. Keep Medicare enrollment distinct from Social Security claiming. Disability, critical illness, accident and hospital indemnity have different triggers and dollar limits. Explain how supplemental payments fit within cash reserves.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An employee with medical coverage buys a policy paying $200 per qualifying hospital day. A five-day covered stay pays $1,000 under the assumed terms. That amount can offset expenses but the medical policy’s deductible and coinsurance still apply.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What should you compare before moving from employer coverage to Medicare?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Enrollment timing, coordination rules, network and drug coverage, total cost and any gaps.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"capstone","title":"The Evans health coverage transition","html":"<h3>The Evans health coverage transition</h3><p>An employee is losing employer coverage. The first alternative costs $450 per month with $6,400 in-network out-of-pocket maximum; the second costs $620 with $3,200 maximum. The employee takes a specialty drug and wants to keep a particular hospital.</p><h4>Your assignment</h4><p>Compare modeled high-use cost, identify what you need to verify before enrolling, and make a timeline that minimizes gaps.</p><details><summary>Reveal the worked solution</summary><p>Modeled premium plus maximum is $11,800 versus $10,640. The second is $1,160 lower in the stated high-use scenario. Verify plan eligibility and effective dates, drug tiers and authorization, hospital and clinicians, and excluded costs. Compare continuation and Marketplace options based on actual eligibility and documents.</p></details><h4>Self-assessment</h4><ol><li>Check that the facts and assumptions are stated.</li><li>Show every calculation and verify the units.</li><li>Compare an alternative and explain what would change your choice.</li><li>Assign the next action and document its owner.</li></ol>"},{"id":"workbook","title":"Advisor worksheet and study plan","html":"<h3>Advisor worksheet and study plan</h3><p>Copy these fields into the fictional case file. Record source, date, responsible person and unresolved items for every entry.</p><p>Covered people, coverage end date, prescriptions, provider access, expected and stress costs, enrollment evidence</p><p>Use one pass to study each module and do its checks. Use a second pass to solve the capstone without notes and explain every missed graded question. Use the issuer’s current course and exam details when planning formal enrollment.</p>"}]','[{"prompt":"What is the high-use modeled cost difference?","choices":["They are equal","Plan B is $2,500 higher","Plan B is $1,000 lower under the assumptions","Plan A is $1,500 lower"]},{"prompt":"What does stop-loss typically protect under the contract?","choices":["Every participant against their personal deductible","The broker from licensing requirements","The network from out-of-network bills","The employer or plan sponsor against specified claim exposure"]},{"prompt":"Which metric isolates the pricing and mix change more effectively than total premium alone?","choices":["Cost per enrolled employee, alongside a normalized benefits comparison","Number of payroll dates","Total premium alone","Carrier brand"]},{"prompt":"What should be verified before telling the employee when coverage will begin?","choices":["Only the date the advisor was called","The qualifying event, each option’s enrollment rules and its actual effective date","Only the preferred premium","Only the employee’s prior copay"]},{"prompt":"What if expected covered-service savings fall to $1,000?","choices":["The options cost the same","It always saves $2,000","The higher-premium option costs $680 more before other differences","The higher-premium option saves $680"]},{"prompt":"What is the coinsurance percent on the allowed amount?","choices":["12%","48%","80%","20%"]},{"prompt":"What should you compare before moving from employer coverage to Medicare?","choices":["Enrollment timing, coordination rules, network and drug coverage, total cost and any gaps","Only the previous payroll deduction","Only the Medicare card color","Only age without checking effective dates or employer circumstances"]}]','CDABCDA'),
('cebs-us-prep','Employee Benefits and Retirement Plans · U.S. CEBS Prep','[{"id":"start","title":"Start here and see the learning path","html":"<h3>Start here and see the learning path</h3><p>This track follows the U.S. five-course CEBS structure. GBA and RPA each use their two subject courses plus the shared GBA/RPA 3 course.</p><p>Begin with the first module. Read the instruction and work the example before opening its solution. Apply the same steps to the new question, then complete the case and submit your graded review. Academy study is independent. Official coursework, exams, experience and designation decisions belong to the issuing organization.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#module-1\">GBA 1 · Group benefits design</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-2\">GBA 2 · Group benefits administration</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-3\">GBA/RPA 3 · Strategic benefits management</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-4\">RPA 1 · Retirement plan design</a></li><li><a href=\"#module-5\">RPA 2 · Retirement plan management</a></li></ol><p><a href=\"#videos\">Watch and discuss</a> · <a href=\"#capstone\">Applied case</a> · <a href=\"#workbook\">Advisor worksheet</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.ifebp.org/cebs/home/courses-and-materials/courses-united-states\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official credential curriculum</a> · <a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/resource-center/faqs/retirement-plans-and-erisa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Practice reference</a></p>"},{"id":"videos","title":"Watch and discuss","html":"<h3>Watch and discuss</h3><p><a href=\"https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/about-ebsa/our-activities/programs-and-initiatives/outreach-and-education/hbec\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">U.S. Department of Labor · Health benefits webinars</a></p><p>Use the webinar section to identify sponsor and administrator responsibilities.</p>"},{"id":"module-1","title":"GBA 1 · Group benefits design","html":"<h3>GBA 1 · Group benefits design</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-1-1\">Design a group medical and welfare package</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-1-2\">Compare insured and self-funded claims</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-1-1","title":"Design a group medical and welfare package","html":"<h3>Design a group medical and welfare package</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Start with eligibility, census, enrollment and employer goals. Compare covered populations, network, prescription benefits, cost-sharing and contribution strategy. Include dental, vision, disability and life as distinct lines with distinct triggers. Stress both employee access and employer budget.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An employer pays 75% of a $640 monthly employee-only premium for 50 enrolled people. Employer cost is $24,000 monthly, or $288,000 yearly before changes or other fees. Workers collectively pay $8,000 monthly. Model alternative contribution splits and effects on participation.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>If the employer pays 70% instead, what is its monthly cost under the same assumptions?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$22,400. Multiply 50 × $640 × 70%.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-1-2","title":"Compare insured and self-funded claims","html":"<h3>Compare insured and self-funded claims</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>A fully insured sponsor pays premium under the contract and the insurer assumes covered claim risk. A self-funded sponsor takes responsibility for covered claims and may contract with an administrator, network and stop-loss carrier. Review fees, stop-loss exclusions, claim run-out and the effect of unexpectedly high claims.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>Expected claim cost is $500,000, administration $60,000 and stop-loss premium $90,000. The initial estimate is $650,000, before reserves or deviations. Add a shock scenario and compare it with a $700,000 guaranteed premium under equivalent coverage assumptions.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What should be included if the sponsor changes administrators before all incurred claims are paid?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Run-out obligations, payment timing and contractual responsibilities.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-2","title":"GBA 2 · Group benefits administration","html":"<h3>GBA 2 · Group benefits administration</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-1\">Administer eligibility, notices and claims</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-2-2\">Make health and disability claims operational</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-2-1","title":"Administer eligibility, notices and claims","html":"<h3>Administer eligibility, notices and claims</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Build a timeline around enrollment, termination, coverage changes, plan documents and participant notices. Identify applicable ERISA, COBRA, HIPAA and ACA obligations based on employer and plan facts. Keep a source and reviewer for each requirement, and reconcile eligibility between payroll, carrier and administrator.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>An enrolled employee leaves the company. Determine the last covered date using plan terms, confirm notice responsibility and delivery, and reconcile the last payroll deduction. Route a disputed termination or claim to the plan’s process and document resolution.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>Which document commonly explains eligibility, benefits and participants’ claims procedures for an ERISA plan?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>The summary plan description, read with plan terms and applicable rules.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-2-2","title":"Make health and disability claims operational","html":"<h3>Make health and disability claims operational</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Map where claims go, what records establish eligibility, who issues determinations and what appeal process applies. Distinguish plan sponsor, insurer, third-party administrator and participant roles. Track privacy and deadlines without circulating unnecessary medical details.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A carrier file shows termination and payroll shows a deduction. Reconcile source data and actual plan eligibility, document the correction, then coordinate any claim reprocessing. Verify notice and appeal obligations separately.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What should be corrected first?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>The underlying eligibility record using source documents and plan rules.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-3","title":"GBA/RPA 3 · Strategic benefits management","html":"<h3>GBA/RPA 3 · Strategic benefits management</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-3-1\">Manage benefits strategically</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-3-2\">Audit benefits communications and data</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-3-1","title":"Manage benefits strategically","html":"<h3>Manage benefits strategically</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Translate business and employee goals into measurable criteria, such as affordability, access, enrollment and participant outcomes. Compare vendors using normalized costs, contract guarantees, service metrics and data ownership. Establish a committee calendar, conflict handling and an action log.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A renewal increases from $650,000 to $715,000. The increase is 10%. Evaluate whether change is driven by enrollment, unit price, benefits or claims. Compare alternatives against the same population and scope. Document which trade-offs leadership accepts.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>If coverage stays constant but enrollment increases 5%, how would you better compare prices?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Calculate cost per enrolled employee as well as total cost.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-3-2","title":"Audit benefits communications and data","html":"<h3>Audit benefits communications and data</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Use a benefits calendar to control renewal, eligibility, payroll, required disclosures and vendor performance. Assign each event an owner, due date and evidence. Where participants need to act, measure whether the message reached them and whether enrollment records match.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A sponsor enrolls 180 of 200 eligible employees. Five payroll discrepancies are found. Initial participation is 90%; discrepancies affect 2.78% of enrolled people if all five are unique. Investigate errors before comparing enrollment between vendors.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is participation if 176 of 200 eligible employees enroll?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>88%. Divide enrolled by eligible.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-4","title":"RPA 1 · Retirement plan design","html":"<h3>RPA 1 · Retirement plan design</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-4-1\">Design retirement plan and contribution rules</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-4-1","title":"Design retirement plan and contribution rules","html":"<h3>Design retirement plan and contribution rules</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Compare defined contribution and defined benefit structures. Assess eligible employees, pay definition, matching, vesting, defaults, employer budget and administration. Model a business owner’s objective alongside costs for the broader employee group. Check current legal limits and plan terms before implementation.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>With a dollar-for-dollar match up to 3% of $70,000, a 3% deferral earns $2,100 under the assumptions. The sponsor’s projected total cost must use eligible pay and likely participation across the workforce.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What match is earned on a 2% deferral under the stated formula?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>$1,400.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"module-5","title":"RPA 2 · Retirement plan management","html":"<h3>RPA 2 · Retirement plan management</h3><p>Study the lessons in order. For each example write the decision, input assumptions and the evidence you would request before advising.</p><ol><li><a href=\"#lesson-5-1\">Monitor plan investments and operations</a></li><li><a href=\"#lesson-5-2\">Compare a pension position with participant accounts</a></li></ol>"},{"id":"lesson-5-1","title":"Monitor plan investments and operations","html":"<h3>Monitor plan investments and operations</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>Define fiduciary roles and document selection and monitoring of investments and providers. Compare fees, diversification, share classes and benchmark fit. Review eligibility audits, contributions, distributions and corrections using plan records. Evaluate pension funding and investment risks independently of participant-directed accounts.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>A vendor quotes $60,000 annually for 200 participants. That is $300 per participant before investment costs. A second quote lists $210 per participant plus asset-based fees. Obtain all-in cost, scope, service standards and conflicts before selecting either.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is the strongest basis for documenting a provider comparison?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>Comparable total fees, scope, quality and the committee’s prudent decision process.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"lesson-5-2","title":"Compare a pension position with participant accounts","html":"<h3>Compare a pension position with participant accounts</h3><h4>Learn the method</h4><p>A defined benefit plan manages assets against promised benefits, assumptions and sponsor contributions. A defined contribution plan measures assets and benefits in participant accounts. For a pension, compare the defined valuation measure and date, review assumptions and ask the actuary before drawing conclusions from a ratio.</p><h4>Work through the example</h4><p>For an illustrative pension, $8 million of assets and $10 million of measured liabilities yield 80% under the given basis. A change in rate assumptions alters liabilities; the ratio does not predict required contributions by itself.</p><h4>Try a new scenario</h4><p>What is the ratio if liabilities rise to $12.5 million and assets remain $8 million?</p><details><summary>Check your work</summary><p>64%, using the provided measurement basis.</p></details><h4>Apply it to practice</h4><p>Write the calculation or client decision. Name one document that verifies an input and one changed fact that would change the result.</p>"},{"id":"capstone","title":"The Mesa employer benefits renewal","html":"<h3>The Mesa employer benefits renewal</h3><p>A sponsor has 80 health plan enrollees, $600 monthly premium per enrollee and a 75% employer contribution. Renewal pricing rises 12% at unchanged enrollment and contribution share. A self-funded proposal estimates $450,000 in aggregate employer cost. The retirement plan vendor reports a separate $50,000 fee for 250 participants.</p><h4>Your assignment</h4><p>Compare medical employer cost and model a 20% self-funded overrun. Calculate retirement fee per participant and create a governance recommendation.</p><details><summary>Reveal the worked solution</summary><p>Current medical employer cost is $432,000, and renewal is $483,840. Self-funding at estimate saves $33,840, but at 20% overrun costs $540,000, or $56,160 more than renewal. Validate benefits, contributions, risk and contract terms before comparing. Retirement administration costs $200 per participant before investment and other fees. Document the committee’s review and ownership of follow-up.</p></details><h4>Self-assessment</h4><ol><li>Check that the facts and assumptions are stated.</li><li>Show every calculation and verify the units.</li><li>Compare an alternative and explain what would change your choice.</li><li>Assign the next action and document its owner.</li></ol>"},{"id":"workbook","title":"Advisor worksheet and study plan","html":"<h3>Advisor worksheet and study plan</h3><p>Copy these fields into the fictional case file. Record source, date, responsible person and unresolved items for every entry.</p><p>Plan type, sponsor and vendor roles, eligible and enrolled headcount, all-in cost, compliance owner and committee decision</p><p>Use one pass to study each module and do its checks. Use a second pass to solve the capstone without notes and explain every missed graded question. Use the issuer’s current course and exam details when planning formal enrollment.</p>"}]','[{"prompt":"If the employer pays 70% instead, what is its monthly cost under the same assumptions?","choices":["$9,600","$32,000","$22,400","$24,000"]},{"prompt":"What should be included if the sponsor changes administrators before all incurred claims are paid?","choices":["Only next year’s enrollment count","Only the old deductible","No costs after plan year end","Run-out obligations, payment timing and contractual responsibilities"]},{"prompt":"Which document commonly explains eligibility, benefits and participants’ claims procedures for an ERISA plan?","choices":["The summary plan description, read with plan terms and applicable rules","A broker’s proposal alone","An employee’s payroll stub","The carrier logo page"]},{"prompt":"What should be corrected first?","choices":["The plan’s deductible","The underlying eligibility record using source documents and plan rules","The insurer logo on the card","Employee premium by guess"]},{"prompt":"If coverage stays constant but enrollment increases 5%, how would you better compare prices?","choices":["Treat the entire increase as price","Ignore enrollment changes","Calculate cost per enrolled employee as well as total cost","Compare totals only"]},{"prompt":"What is participation if 176 of 200 eligible employees enroll?","choices":["90%","82%","92%","88%"]},{"prompt":"What match is earned on a 2% deferral under the stated formula?","choices":["$1,400","$700","$2,100","$3,500"]},{"prompt":"What is the strongest basis for documenting a provider comparison?","choices":["A firm’s referral payment","Comparable total fees, scope, quality and the committee’s prudent decision process","The lowest sticker price alone","The highest past fund return alone"]},{"prompt":"What is the ratio if liabilities rise to $12.5 million and assets remain $8 million?","choices":["62.5%","156.25%","64%, using the provided measurement basis","80%"]}]','CDABCDABC');
